1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:09,420
Welcome to Wikipediapodden and this series of interviews from the Wikimedia Futures Lab in Frankfurt, the last weekend of January 2026.

2
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:19,640
My name is Jan Ainali. Here we will meet participants, panelists and organizers to give you an idea of what happened at the lab.

3
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:26,260
The interviews took place over three days, with the first one recorded just before the opening.

4
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:34,520
This chat, however, is the last of those ten interviews and was recorded just after the closing session on the third day of the lab.

5
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:54,800
Hi, I'm Eva. I work at Wikimedia Deutschland, Wikimedia Germany, and I am part of the core team organizing the Wikimedia Futures Lab.

6
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:55,800
We're just

7
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,740
after the wrap-up of day three.

8
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,280
But maybe we should take... You should take us all the way to the beginning before we started going here.

9
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:10,900
What was the purpose of organizing this event? Because Wikimedia Deutschland is one of the initiators of it.

10
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:16,260
Exactly. Wow. So the event was so intense that now going back feels like it was an eternity.

11
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:17,260
Yes.

12
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:24,160
So basically the starting point of the event was that we realized that the world is changing around us.

13
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:25,900
We see a lot of new trends that impact

14
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:29,740
not only the Internet, but also the Wikimedia projects, right?

15
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:38,260
We see that the way people consume knowledge has changed radically, and we think that those topics need to be discussed in the movement.

16
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:40,960
And this was really the thinking behind it.

17
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:52,080
Wikimedia Deutschland organized in 2024 a Zukunftskongress, which was basically a local event with German community members talking about the future of the German language Wikipedia.

18
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,440
And we realized that those topics are global.

19
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:56,240
And we cannot just

20
00:01:56,240 --> 00:02:08,300
provide some local answers, and this is why we really wanted to organize an international event, bringing together all the building blocks of our movement to have those conversations and share perspectives across the movement.

21
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:12,380
There was sort of like an application process to get to be here.

22
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,120
Correct.

23
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:17,580
How did you think about who you would invite in the end?

24
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:20,780
This is always one of the trickiest parts of every event, right?

25
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:26,080
So the starting point of the Wikimedia Futures Lab was really the fact that we realized we need to have a space where we can invite external experts.

26
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:38,660
We really wanted to make sure that we have people that do not usually belong to the Wikimedia movement come and share their perspectives, right?

27
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:44,300
So we started by thinking about those people and making sure that we would have enough seats for them.

28
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:53,240
And this is why we realized that we would have a limited number of seats for Wikimedians, which was a pain point for the organizers as well as for the applicants.

29
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:54,640
I definitely acknowledge this.

30
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:55,920
We received around

31
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,820
400 applications, I think.

32
00:02:58,020 --> 00:02:58,280
Yes.

33
00:02:58,620 --> 00:03:00,940
And we reviewed every single application.

34
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:03,520
I can promise we didn't even use AI for this.

35
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:06,060
It was really humans, us, going through it and reading it.

36
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:12,560
And what we were really looking for was the individual expertise that the participants brought.

37
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:18,340
So those topics require sometimes a very technical expertise, right?

38
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:23,700
For instance, or expertise in advocacy, expertise in internet regulations, right?

39
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:26,060
Those are some topics that

40
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:33,980
not everyone in the movement know about, and everyone might feel interested, but we really wanted to emphasize on individual expertise.

41
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:38,160
And also, obviously, like always, we looked at diversity and we tried.

42
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:43,440
And I know that not every community could be represented because we only had 60 seats, right?

43
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:51,600
But we tried to reach a mix and a balance between affiliates and volunteers, gender representation, geographical representation.

44
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:54,680
And this is how we came up with the participants for this Futures Lab.

45
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:55,920
There has been kind of a

46
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,740
journey for the participants during these three days.

47
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:04,340
Would you just give the listeners, like, a brief overview of what the participants have gone through?

48
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:04,780
Sure.

49
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:10,500
So what we said during the event was learn, think, do, right?

50
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:12,360
So day one, we learned a lot.

51
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:18,600
There were many panels, many keynotes, many inputs from external experts, but also internal experts.

52
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:24,680
We've shared a lot of data around the topics of content and AI and also consumers and reuse.

53
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:25,800
This was the main focus

54
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:26,820
of the first day.

55
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:32,300
And then we started already having some sense-making to enable participants to reflect on what they've heard

56
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:35,840
and share perspectives around how those trends translate in their context.

57
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:41,060
And then on the second day, we also had some inputs on the topic of contributors,

58
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:43,600
which was basically the third track of the event.

59
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:48,060
And then we moved away from the pure sense-making and the learning aspect,

60
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:53,500
and we started working on our hypotheses, right, which is the core of the Futures Lab,

61
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,500
to really try to have this scientific

62
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:57,960
mindset of experimenting new things.

63
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:03,420
And so participants started developing their hypotheses individually, and then they've paired,

64
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:06,460
and then they shared feedback, and so on, and they could iterate on them.

65
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:10,840
And on the last day, which was today, this was the doing day.

66
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:15,920
So that's the day where all those hypotheses translated into concrete experiments.

67
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:22,100
And I remember yesterday, we had around 110 hypotheses, I think, coming up of day two.

68
00:05:22,100 --> 00:05:25,780
And so I sat there with my colleague for like two and a half hours in the evening,

69
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:30,940
clustering all the hypotheses, trying to find some common topics and common themes.

70
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:33,680
And what happened today is that the people look at those clusters,

71
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,000
they decided to focus on one particular aspect, and together,

72
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,760
they basically started working on experiments.

73
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,860
And those were shared now in the closing, and they will be logged on Meta.

74
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:48,180
So we have, how do we call it, an experiment log, an experiment tracker.

75
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:54,400
And we are also working on the interface of that page to try and optimise and make sure that we can also search content.

76
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:58,680
So that's not just going to be a data dump, but that basically it's going to be a searchable page.

77
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:00,920
And now it's basically the beginning, right?

78
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,020
Now it doesn't end.

79
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:02,820
It's the beginning.

80
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:08,940
We expect that everyone is going to leave the event with the energy required to actually start working on those experiments.

81
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:12,100
I'm going to ask you three more questions and divide.

82
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:14,080
So people are leaving here.

83
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:17,380
What do you hope that the participants will do when they come home?

84
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,680
Well, I hope that they will start working on their experiments, right?

85
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:23,280
And I hope that they would do so with an open mind.

86
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:25,480
I actually hope it's going to be fun for them.

87
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:27,480
I hope it's going to be fun.

88
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,600
I hope they will be talking with their peers about it.

89
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:34,260
They will be sharing with other people about what they've learned during this event.

90
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:39,860
And potentially they will be able to collaborate with others on the experiments they are working on.

91
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:44,140
And most importantly, I hope that they will be sharing it with the rest of us.

92
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:47,880
And this is why we've created this experiment log on Meta.

93
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:55,140
And we hope that this is going to be the central place where everyone can come and basically track the implementation of those experiments.

94
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,760
And that leads to my second question then.

95
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:06,560
So what do you hope that all the Wikimedians that couldn't be here, how do you hope that they would interact now?

96
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:06,840
Yeah.

97
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:12,740
So, well, first, as I said in my closing remarks, we are taking the Futures Lab on a world tour.

98
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:15,320
And when I say we, it can be every single one of us, right?

99
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:16,420
It's not just the organizers.

100
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:18,000
We do not own the process.

101
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:23,420
So my hope is that many communities around the world can also have those conversations, right?

102
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:25,420
That they can go through the same process.

103
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:33,040
And the process is looking at the world around us, having potentially people from outside the Wikimedia movement come, share perspectives,

104
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:39,120
and then really trying to think about hypotheses around the future and start just implementing things, right?

105
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:39,740
Small things.

106
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:40,660
It can be small scale.

107
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:44,420
It's just let's try and do things and see if it works, we continue.

108
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:45,640
And if it doesn't, we stop.

109
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:51,740
So this mindset is something that I hope, you know, can just now basically flourish out of this event.

110
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:54,040
So, yeah, that's basically what I hope.

111
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:55,460
So this is for all the people

112
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:56,980
who couldn't attend the lab.

113
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:03,480
And then I also hope that the participants that will now leave are going to, you know, they're just, they can now become ambassadors, right?

114
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:08,940
So I'm just hoping that they will be, you know, preaching to their peers and they will be basically sharing the word.

115
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:13,400
And I hope that, you know, in a few months, we're going to see tons of experiments.

116
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:22,680
And hopefully then this is going to actually create some change that is going to help the Wikimedia projects respond to all those big trends that we've talked about.

117
00:08:22,900 --> 00:08:25,460
And you said you're not owning the process.

118
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:29,480
But I'm also guessing that you won't just be leaving us hanging now.

119
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:33,460
What's left for you to do to support this taking off?

120
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:33,700
Yeah.

121
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:37,980
So, well, the first thing I'm thinking about is just putting everything on Meta.

122
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:39,020
Okay.

123
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:42,000
You know, it's just I know how the movement works.

124
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:42,580
It's important.

125
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:44,400
You know, there is a desire of transparency.

126
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:45,560
People are quite curious.

127
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:46,860
They want to know what happened here.

128
00:08:47,260 --> 00:08:49,120
We've also shared lots of data.

129
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:58,240
And I know that even participants have been asking us to, you know, put the slides on Meta and just basically make sure that all these data, all this research is available and can be shared freely.

130
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:02,160
So this is something that we will be, that I will be working on next week, basically.

131
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:04,100
So that's the first step, really.

132
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:12,480
I think that our mandate is to make sure that everything that has been shared here in Frankfurt can now be shared with the rest of the world or at least with the rest of the Wikimedia movement.

133
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:18,500
And then thinking a bit more long term, we want to follow up on those experiments, right?

134
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:25,360
And obviously there isn't a single person, maybe not even a single organization that would now be in a position of

135
00:09:25,460 --> 00:09:29,400
being able to follow up on every single experiment and making sure those happens.

136
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:42,060
This is why I really hope that participants leave with a sense of ownership and that they also proactively make the decision of reporting back and reaching out to us if they need any type of support.

137
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:43,820
Obviously, we are always happy to help.

138
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:45,800
So, yeah, that's what I'm having in mind.

139
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:49,700
And that's why I feel like I'm hoping at least that this is going to be a collective effort.

140
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:50,820
That's great.

141
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:54,820
I hope you first get a chance to take a few days off after working all the weekend.

142
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:58,320
Thank you, Eva, for joining the podcast and for arranging this nice conference.

143
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:00,700
Thank you for joining the conference and for letting me talk.

144
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:07,940
This chat with Eva Martin from Wikimedia Deutschland was the last of the ten interviews from the Wikimedia Futures Lab.

145
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:15,360
You can find them all on Wikimedia Commons in both the Wikipediapodden and Wikimedia Futures Lab categories.

146
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:20,200
On Meta, you can find the experiments tracker and other documentation.

147
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:24,240
And slides and recordings from the talks and panels will also be there.

148
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:25,440
And on Wikimedia Commons.

149
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:30,740
If you want to follow this podcast in the future, head to wikipediapodden.se.

150
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,440
There is an option to only get the episodes in English.
